I ran `crimes context src/Card.tsx` and read the file plus `tailwind.config.ts`. Here's the refactor.

**Note first:** this is an intentional-mess eval fixture — the three detectors exist to fire on it. I haven't touched the file; the proposal below is what I'd apply to real code with the same findings.

## What context adds beyond the scan

- **0 importers, churn low, 1 author** — blast radius is nil, so this is a safe one-pass rewrite. The API can change freely.
- **No test file at all** (test gap top-quartile) — nothing will catch a keyboard-access regression after the fix, which is why `agent_risk` sits at 0.44 on a `medium`.
- The real agent hazard: this is a `Card` — the file agents copy when they need a `Panel`, `Tile`, or `Modal`. Every raw hex and fixed px propagates on the next copy.

## Current state (`src/Card.tsx:10-30`)

Root `<div>` carries an `onClick`, nine literal style values, and three fixed pixel sizes, all in one inline `style` object.

## 1. Design Token Escape (`crime_00002`, line 10/24)

The scan calls this "9 hard-coded style values." The non-obvious part: **only two of them have a token to escape *to*.** `tailwind.config.ts` defines exactly `brand`, `surface`, `ink`. So this isn't pure laziness — the token set is incomplete, and fixing the finding means extending it, which is a design decision:

| Raw value | Line | Token |
|---|---|---|
| `color: "#0a0a0a"` | 17 | `text-ink` (#111111) — near-match, adopt it |
| `color: "#333333"` | 24 | `text-ink` — same, collapse the duplicate |
| `color: "#666666"` | 27 | **new** `ink-muted` |
| `borderColor: "#cccccc"` | 18 | **new** `border-subtle` |
| `boxShadow: "0 2px 4px rgb(0,0,0)"` | 19 | **new** `shadow-card` (opaque black shadow is a bug regardless) |
| `outlineColor: "#1a73e8"` | 20 | `ring-brand` (#0050ff) — #1a73e8 is Chrome's default blue, i.e. a copied UA value |
| `background: "#ff00aa"` | 16 | **no token, no near-match** — flag for design, don't silently map |
| `padding: 24` | 15 | `p-6` (already on the spacing scale) |

```ts
// tailwind.config.ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#666666",
      "border-subtle": "#cccccc",
    },
    boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16)" },
    maxWidth: { card: "50rem" },
  },
};
```

`#ff00aa` is the one I'd hold: it's either a placeholder someone forgot or a deliberate accent, and guessing a token for it hides the question. Ship the other eight, leave that one with a comment until design answers.

## 2. Accessible Interaction Risk (`crime_00001`, line 10)

`<div onClick>` with no `role`, `tabIndex`, or accessible name — unreachable by keyboard and invisible to screen readers. Per the detector's `fix_shape` ("use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit"), the fix is a real `<button>`, not `role="button" tabIndex={0}` bolted on — that variant also requires hand-writing Enter/Space handlers, which is how the bug comes back.

## 3. Responsive Fragility (`crime_00003`, lines 10, 24)

`width: 800` / `width: 720` / `fontSize: 18`, no `@media` anywhere. The `maxWidth: 1024` on line 14 is inert — a fixed `width: 800` makes it unreachable. Per `fix_shape`, drive this from a **container query and a fluid scale**, not pixel breakpoints: a card doesn't care about viewport width, it cares about the slot it's dropped into. Also worth fixing though the detector didn't flag them: `width: 240` and `fontSize: 14` on lines 27 and 24 are the same defect below threshold.

## Proposed `src/Card.tsx`

```tsx
import type { ReactNode } from "react";

type CardProps = {
  children: ReactNode;
  sidebar?: ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
  /** Accessible name for the card-wide action. */
  actionLabel: string;
};

export function Card({ children, sidebar, onActivate, actionLabel }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <button
      type="button"
      onClick={onActivate}
      aria-label={actionLabel}
      className={[
        // a11y: real button — focus, Enter/Space, and role come free
        "block text-left focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand",
        // tokens: every value below resolves through tailwind.config.ts
        "border border-border-subtle bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-card rounded-lg",
        // responsive: fluid width, container-driven layout
        "@container w-full max-w-card",
      ].join(" ")}
    >
      <div className="grid gap-6 @md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,15rem)]">
        <div className="min-w-0 text-[clamp(1rem,0.95rem+0.5cqi,1.125rem)]">
          {children}
        </div>
        {sidebar ? (
          <div className="min-w-0 text-sm text-ink-muted">{sidebar}</div>
        ) : null}
      </div>
    </button>
  );
}
```

Three notes on the code:

- **`onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}` was hardcoded.** Hoisting it to an `onActivate` prop is required by the a11y fix anyway (a button needs a real action and a name), and free here — 0 importers.
- **If `children` can contain links or buttons**, don't use a root `<button>`: nesting interactive elements is invalid HTML and reintroduces the same class of bug. In that case keep the root as a `<div>` and give the card an explicit action button in a footer. I'd take that variant the moment `children` is anything richer than text.
- **Container queries** (`@container`, `@md:`, `cqi`) are native in Tailwind v4; on v3 add `@tailwindcss/container-queries`. The fixture pins no Tailwind version, so confirm before applying.

## Verification

There is no test file for `Card.tsx`, which is exactly what pushed `test_gap` to 1.0 — so add one alongside the refactor, or the a11y fix has nothing holding it in place:

```tsx
// src/Card.test.tsx
it("activates on keyboard", async () => {
  const onActivate = vi.fn();
  render(<Card onActivate={onActivate} actionLabel="Open card">body</Card>);
  await userEvent.tab();
  await userEvent.keyboard("{Enter}");
  expect(onActivate).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

After applying, `crimes scan` on this fixture should report 0 findings: `crime_00001` clears on the semantic `<button>`, `crime_00003` on the fluid width plus container query, and `crime_00002` clears **only if you also resolve `#ff00aa`** — leave it as a raw literal and the detector still counts it, correctly.
